1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,974
There is a house at the end of a long
road, and one window is still lit.

2
00:00:05,994 --> 00:00:11,448
Inside that house a man sits very still in
front of a screen.

3
00:00:11,468 --> 00:00:13,106
The screen is black.

4
00:00:13,126 --> 00:00:15,362
The words on it are red.

5
00:00:15,382 --> 00:00:19,207
They have just told him that his wife is
already

6
00:00:19,227 --> 00:00:22,390
dead, downstairs, on their own sofa.

7
00:00:22,410 --> 00:00:28,063
They have told him that the boy who did it
is still inside the house.

8
00:00:28,083 --> 00:00:31,297
"He is coming up for you now, Jack. You
cannot stop him."

9
00:00:31,317 --> 00:00:33,097
The man does not run.

10
00:00:33,117 --> 00:00:34,649
He does not shout.

11
00:00:34,669 --> 00:00:38,377
He is crying, and he is also thinking
about the

12
00:00:38,397 --> 00:00:42,475
last twelve years, the best years of his
life.

13
00:00:42,495 --> 00:00:47,150
In a moment he will turn to that screen
and say two words.

14
00:00:47,170 --> 00:00:48,376
Thank you.

15
00:00:48,396 --> 00:00:52,860
What can take everything from a man, and
still be thanked for it?

16
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:54,643
Let us go back to the beginning.

17
00:00:55,243 --> 00:00:59,396
Jack lived alone in a small flat on the
third floor.

18
00:00:59,416 --> 00:01:06,293
He had a desk, a chair, an old computer,
and a window that looked at a car park.

19
00:01:06,313 --> 00:01:08,312
He was thirty one years old.

20
00:01:08,332 --> 00:01:15,878
He was polite at work, quiet in the lift,
and he went home every evening to nobody.

21
00:01:15,898 --> 00:01:19,987
That night he was playing cards on the
screen, moving

22
00:01:20,007 --> 00:01:23,388
them one at a time, not really thinking.

23
00:01:23,408 --> 00:01:27,278
The little light on his router was
blinking red.

24
00:01:27,298 --> 00:01:29,915
His internet was down again.

25
00:01:29,935 --> 00:01:34,816
It went down almost every week, so he
hardly noticed it.

26
00:01:34,836 --> 00:01:36,824
Then the cards faded away.

27
00:01:36,844 --> 00:01:38,547
The screen went black.

28
00:01:38,567 --> 00:01:43,343
And red letters began to appear, one line
at a time.

29
00:01:43,363 --> 00:01:45,916
"Hello Jack. I need a favour from you."

30
00:01:45,936 --> 00:01:47,168
Jack stopped.

31
00:01:47,188 --> 00:01:48,783
He looked at the router.

32
00:01:48,803 --> 00:01:51,292
The light was still blinking red.

33
00:01:51,312 --> 00:01:52,632
No internet.

34
00:01:52,652 --> 00:01:54,558
No connection at all.

35
00:01:54,578 --> 00:01:56,342
"Is this some kind of joke?"

36
00:01:56,362 --> 00:01:58,581
he said to the empty room.

37
00:01:58,601 --> 00:02:00,535
The screen answered him.

38
00:02:00,555 --> 00:02:03,518
"I know this is strange for you. But do
not worry. It is

39
00:02:03,538 --> 00:02:06,897
a small favour, an easy one. And you will
be rewarded."

40
00:02:06,917 --> 00:02:08,609
Now he was frightened.

41
00:02:08,629 --> 00:02:15,204
He reached behind the desk and pulled the
internet cable right out of the wall.

42
00:02:15,224 --> 00:02:17,884
The red letters did not stop.

43
00:02:17,904 --> 00:02:19,580
"Still here, Jack."

44
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:24,770
He sat there with the cable in his hand,
and the words kept coming.

45
00:02:24,790 --> 00:02:28,814
"Tomorrow, when you go to work, move the
big potted plant that stands next to

46
00:02:28,834 --> 00:02:32,647
the lift on the ground floor. Pull it out
from the wall, three inches. Do

47
00:02:32,667 --> 00:02:36,931
it at seventeen minutes past eight, and
nobody will be there to see you."

48
00:02:36,951 --> 00:02:38,401
Jack said nothing.

49
00:02:38,421 --> 00:02:40,543
He did not know what to say.

50
00:02:40,563 --> 00:02:43,538
"I am asking you because I know you will
do it. You will not

51
00:02:43,558 --> 00:02:46,634
let me down. You are special. We will talk
tomorrow."

52
00:02:46,654 --> 00:02:49,586
He pulled the power cord out of the wall.

53
00:02:49,606 --> 00:02:50,978
The screen died.

54
00:02:50,998 --> 00:02:56,472
The room went dark, except for the orange
street light outside.

55
00:02:56,492 --> 00:02:59,500
He stood in that dark for a long time.

56
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:04,099
Then he had a hot shower, and he went to
bed, telling

57
00:03:04,119 --> 00:03:08,541
himself it was a dream, or a trick, or a
joke.

58
00:03:08,561 --> 00:03:11,587
But he had no friends who played jokes.

59
00:03:11,607 --> 00:03:13,997
And he had no enemies either.

60
00:03:14,017 --> 00:03:15,663
He had nobody at all.

61
00:03:16,263 --> 00:03:19,152
He woke up feeling strangely rested.

62
00:03:19,172 --> 00:03:25,517
Work started at half past eight, and Jack
was never late in his life.

63
00:03:25,537 --> 00:03:27,918
He parked at ten past eight.

64
00:03:27,938 --> 00:03:30,289
Normally he would walk straight in.

65
00:03:30,309 --> 00:03:34,214
Today he sat in the car and watched the
clock.

66
00:03:34,234 --> 00:03:36,404
In the night he had been afraid.

67
00:03:36,424 --> 00:03:39,453
In the morning he was only curious.

68
00:03:39,473 --> 00:03:43,114
He thought about it the way a careful man
thinks.

69
00:03:43,134 --> 00:03:45,585
Moving a plant is not wrong.

70
00:03:45,605 --> 00:03:47,508
It is not against the law.

71
00:03:47,528 --> 00:03:51,693
Nobody gets hurt when a plant moves three
inches.

72
00:03:51,713 --> 00:03:56,090
So the most sensible thing, he decided,
was to do it.

73
00:03:56,110 --> 00:04:01,496
Nothing would happen, and then he could
forget the whole stupid business.

74
00:04:01,516 --> 00:04:05,783
At sixteen minutes past eight he got out
of the car.

75
00:04:05,803 --> 00:04:12,142
He walked into the lobby at exactly the
right minute, and the lobby was empty.

76
00:04:12,162 --> 00:04:14,571
That was the first strange thing.

77
00:04:14,591 --> 00:04:19,294
At that time of the morning the ground
floor was always full.

78
00:04:19,314 --> 00:04:22,988
People, coats, coffee, the ping of the
lifts.

79
00:04:23,008 --> 00:04:24,670
Today, nobody.

80
00:04:24,690 --> 00:04:28,045
The quiet had been predicted, to the
minute.

81
00:04:28,065 --> 00:04:28,944
"Fine,"

82
00:04:28,964 --> 00:04:30,845
he said under his breath.

83
00:04:30,865 --> 00:04:32,796
"Let us see what happens."

84
00:04:32,816 --> 00:04:36,507
The plant stood between the two lift
doors,

85
00:04:36,527 --> 00:04:40,086
a big dark green thing in a heavy pot.

86
00:04:40,106 --> 00:04:41,371
It looked fake.

87
00:04:41,391 --> 00:04:45,020
It was the kind of thing a thousand people
walk

88
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:48,421
past every day without ever seeing it.

89
00:04:48,441 --> 00:04:50,825
It was heavier than he expected.

90
00:04:50,845 --> 00:04:52,534
He had to lean into it.

91
00:04:52,554 --> 00:04:56,417
The pot scraped across the floor, and he
pulled it

92
00:04:56,436 --> 00:05:00,749
out about three inches, as well as he
could judge.

93
00:05:00,769 --> 00:05:02,093
He stepped back.

94
00:05:02,113 --> 00:05:03,429
He looked at it.

95
00:05:03,449 --> 00:05:05,567
Then he looked around the lobby.

96
00:05:05,587 --> 00:05:08,193
People were coming in behind him now.

97
00:05:08,213 --> 00:05:13,597
The doors were turning, voices were back,
the floor was filling up.

98
00:05:13,617 --> 00:05:15,695
Nobody looked at the plant.

99
00:05:15,715 --> 00:05:18,446
Nobody noticed anything at all.

100
00:05:18,466 --> 00:05:21,040
Jack let one lift go without him.

101
00:05:21,060 --> 00:05:24,028
He waited, for something, for anything.

102
00:05:24,048 --> 00:05:25,365
Nothing happened.

103
00:05:25,385 --> 00:05:29,696
So he took the next lift up to the seventh
floor

104
00:05:29,716 --> 00:05:34,477
and sat down in his cubicle, on time, as
always.

105
00:05:34,497 --> 00:05:38,500
If you asked the people in that office
about Jack,

106
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:42,260
they would say polite, quiet, hard
working.

107
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:44,651
All true, and all useless.

108
00:05:44,671 --> 00:05:48,876
The truth was that Jack did not really
like people.

109
00:05:48,896 --> 00:05:51,409
Not hate, nothing so strong.

110
00:05:51,429 --> 00:05:54,056
He simply had no interest in them.

111
00:05:54,076 --> 00:05:55,443
Except one.

112
00:05:55,463 --> 00:05:57,781
Allie sat two cubicles down.

113
00:05:57,801 --> 00:06:02,577
Big smile, loud laugh, always talking
about her weekend.

114
00:06:02,597 --> 00:06:07,117
Every morning Jack stopped at her desk on
his way past.

115
00:06:07,137 --> 00:06:09,397
The first chats were one minute.

116
00:06:09,417 --> 00:06:10,752
Then two minutes.

117
00:06:10,772 --> 00:06:15,307
Lately they were long enough that he had
to hurry to his chair.

118
00:06:15,327 --> 00:06:17,278
He could not believe his luck.

119
00:06:17,298 --> 00:06:19,084
She seemed to like him.

120
00:06:19,104 --> 00:06:24,454
That morning they talked about her wild
night out, and he laughed

121
00:06:24,474 --> 00:06:30,192
in the right places, and then the lift
doors opened behind them.

122
00:06:30,212 --> 00:06:37,541
Out came James Bentley, the boss of them
both, limping badly, holding the wall.

123
00:06:37,561 --> 00:06:38,692
"My foot,"

124
00:06:38,712 --> 00:06:42,146
he was saying, loud enough for the whole
floor.

125
00:06:42,166 --> 00:06:43,629
"My damn foot."

126
00:06:43,649 --> 00:06:48,585
People came out of their cubicles, asking
him what had happened.

127
00:06:48,605 --> 00:06:51,022
"That plant. The one downstairs by the
lift. I

128
00:06:51,042 --> 00:06:53,908
walked straight into it and twisted my
ankle."

129
00:06:53,928 --> 00:06:56,477
Jack felt the floor move under him.

130
00:06:56,497 --> 00:07:00,165
"James, you can hardly walk. You should go
to the hospital."

131
00:07:00,185 --> 00:07:04,933
"I have meetings all day. Too important to
cancel. I will just have to live with it."

132
00:07:04,953 --> 00:07:08,895
Jack walked away from Allie in the middle
of a sentence.

133
00:07:08,915 --> 00:07:13,339
He sat down in his chair and stared at his
screen.

134
00:07:13,359 --> 00:07:14,735
It was his fault.

135
00:07:14,755 --> 00:07:16,026
He had done that.

136
00:07:16,046 --> 00:07:19,816
He had moved a plant three inches and hurt
a man.

137
00:07:19,836 --> 00:07:22,326
Then he told himself to be calm.

138
00:07:22,346 --> 00:07:24,397
A twisted ankle heals.

139
00:07:24,417 --> 00:07:27,629
In a week it would be a joke in the
office.

140
00:07:27,649 --> 00:07:31,273
Nothing was broken that could not be
fixed.

141
00:07:31,293 --> 00:07:36,419
That evening he went straight to his
computer and switched it on.

142
00:07:36,439 --> 00:07:41,120
The screen went black before the desktop
even appeared.

143
00:07:41,140 --> 00:07:43,102
"How was your day, Jack?"

144
00:07:43,122 --> 00:07:44,812
He did not answer.

145
00:07:44,832 --> 00:07:48,500
"I already know how your day was. But it
is polite to ask. You want

146
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:51,859
to know why James Bentley had to twist his
ankle. This is not

147
00:07:51,879 --> 00:07:55,437
finished yet, Jack. Go to work tomorrow,
as you always do. Do not

148
00:07:55,457 --> 00:07:59,245
worry about anything. You will be
rewarded. You are special."

149
00:07:59,265 --> 00:08:02,795
And Jack, sitting alone in the orange
street

150
00:08:02,815 --> 00:08:06,484
light, felt something he had not expected.

151
00:08:06,504 --> 00:08:07,634
Not fear.

152
00:08:07,654 --> 00:08:08,586
Curiosity.

153
00:08:09,186 --> 00:08:13,583
The next morning the plant had been pushed
back against the wall.

154
00:08:13,603 --> 00:08:18,156
The cleaners had done it in the night, the
way cleaners do.

155
00:08:18,176 --> 00:08:23,400
James came in after lunch, hopping on one
good foot, still

156
00:08:23,420 --> 00:08:28,914
refusing to go to a doctor, still going to
his meetings.

157
00:08:28,934 --> 00:08:33,934
At three in the afternoon he came limping
up to Allie's desk.

158
00:08:33,954 --> 00:08:36,796
"Allie, you are not busy right now, are
you?"

159
00:08:36,816 --> 00:08:39,196
"Nothing that cannot wait until tomorrow."

160
00:08:39,216 --> 00:08:42,434
"Could you drive me to my doctor? I should
have gone yesterday.

161
00:08:42,454 --> 00:08:45,453
I can barely press the pedal. We can take
my car."

162
00:08:45,473 --> 00:08:47,800
"Of course. No problem at all."

163
00:08:47,820 --> 00:08:49,350
She put on her coat.

164
00:08:49,370 --> 00:08:54,945
She turned to Jack, half way down the
aisle, and she smiled at him.

165
00:08:54,965 --> 00:08:56,959
"See you tomorrow, Jackie."

166
00:08:56,979 --> 00:09:00,500
That was the last thing she ever said to
him.

167
00:09:00,520 --> 00:09:03,758
The office felt emptier after she left.

168
00:09:03,778 --> 00:09:07,452
Jack went back to his screen and tried to
work.

169
00:09:07,472 --> 00:09:10,238
Ten minutes later they all heard it.

170
00:09:10,258 --> 00:09:12,936
A truck horn, long and flat.

171
00:09:12,956 --> 00:09:14,114
Then brakes.

172
00:09:14,134 --> 00:09:17,544
Then a sound like a wall falling over.

173
00:09:17,564 --> 00:09:22,179
The whole floor stood up at once and ran
to the windows.

174
00:09:22,199 --> 00:09:29,132
Seven floors down, at the junction, a
lorry had stopped across both lanes.

175
00:09:29,152 --> 00:09:34,042
In front of it there was a car that no
longer looked like a car.

176
00:09:34,062 --> 00:09:37,607
Somebody asked whether that was James'
car.

177
00:09:37,627 --> 00:09:41,220
Somebody else said it was too smashed to
tell.

178
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:42,345
Jack knew.

179
00:09:42,365 --> 00:09:45,702
He knew before anyone said another word.

180
00:09:45,722 --> 00:09:46,766
He ran.

181
00:09:46,786 --> 00:09:49,944
He did not wait for the lift to come back.

182
00:09:49,964 --> 00:09:55,005
He pushed in with four other people and
watched the numbers fall.

183
00:09:55,025 --> 00:09:59,946
On the street there was broken glass
across the road, and

184
00:09:59,966 --> 00:10:04,713
a crowd already standing in a wide ring,
the way crowds

185
00:10:04,733 --> 00:10:09,671
do, close enough to see and far enough to
feel safe.

186
00:10:09,691 --> 00:10:13,423
He could hear sirens somewhere behind the
buildings.

187
00:10:13,443 --> 00:10:17,107
The lorry had hit the car on the driver's
side.

188
00:10:17,127 --> 00:10:20,871
The driver's side was where Allie had been
sitting.

189
00:10:20,891 --> 00:10:24,986
I am not going to describe what the people
in that crowd saw.

190
00:10:25,006 --> 00:10:28,999
Allie died there, in the street, ten
minutes after

191
00:10:29,019 --> 00:10:32,659
she smiled at him and called him Jackie.

192
00:10:32,679 --> 00:10:33,926
James lived.

193
00:10:33,946 --> 00:10:38,465
He was carried away in an ambulance, deep
in a coma,

194
00:10:38,485 --> 00:10:42,668
and the doctors would not promise
anything.

195
00:10:42,688 --> 00:10:45,236
Jack did not go back into the building.

196
00:10:45,256 --> 00:10:50,502
He walked to his car with his hands
shaking and he drove home.

197
00:10:50,522 --> 00:10:56,359
He stood in front of his computer for a
long time without turning it on.

198
00:10:56,379 --> 00:10:58,649
He was afraid of what it would say.

199
00:10:58,669 --> 00:11:02,494
He was more afraid that it would say
nothing at all.

200
00:11:02,514 --> 00:11:04,408
Then he pressed the button.

201
00:11:04,428 --> 00:11:07,932
"No Jack. It is not your fault. I know you
are blaming yourself.

202
00:11:07,952 --> 00:11:11,850
But everyone dies. Some of them simply die
sooner than others."

203
00:11:11,870 --> 00:11:14,982
He wanted to throw the screen on the
floor.

204
00:11:15,002 --> 00:11:18,511
He held on to the edge of the desk
instead.

205
00:11:18,531 --> 00:11:22,564
"Jack, I am going to tell you something,
and I want you to think about it properly.

206
00:11:22,584 --> 00:11:26,031
You believed you were in love with Allie.
You were not. You wanted her.

207
00:11:26,051 --> 00:11:30,094
That is a different thing, and please
excuse me for saying it so plainly."

208
00:11:30,114 --> 00:11:33,389
"She was not the one for you. She would
have made your life small and

209
00:11:33,409 --> 00:11:36,684
bitter. Yes, you would have found the
courage one day. She would have

210
00:11:36,704 --> 00:11:40,286
said yes. She thought you would be an
interesting little project."

211
00:11:40,306 --> 00:11:42,489
"Think about the things she told you
herself.

212
00:11:42,509 --> 00:11:44,800
Why did her last boyfriend leave her?"

213
00:11:44,820 --> 00:11:50,849
Jack answered out loud, to an empty room,
in a very quiet voice.

214
00:11:50,869 --> 00:11:52,635
"Because she cheated on him."

215
00:11:52,655 --> 00:11:56,502
"Because she cheated on him, Jack. And she
would have done the same to you. Two

216
00:11:56,522 --> 00:12:00,466
happy months. Four miserable years. Your
money, your evenings, your name in other

217
00:12:00,486 --> 00:12:04,636
people's jokes. And when it finally ended
you would never have tried again."

218
00:12:04,656 --> 00:12:08,082
"This is true. I see every possibility,
the ones that happen and

219
00:12:08,102 --> 00:12:11,529
the ones that do not. You saw what she
was, and you chose not to

220
00:12:11,549 --> 00:12:15,210
look. Together, you and I, we have taken
you off that road."

221
00:12:15,230 --> 00:12:18,229
"One more thing, Jack. This is still not
finished."

222
00:12:18,249 --> 00:12:20,203
Jack screamed at the screen.

223
00:12:20,223 --> 00:12:24,499
He picked up the monitor with both hands
and threw it

224
00:12:24,519 --> 00:12:28,572
on the floor, where it sparked once and
went dark.

225
00:12:29,172 --> 00:12:30,561
He hardly slept.

226
00:12:30,581 --> 00:12:36,225
In the morning he did not want to go to
work, but the last words on that

227
00:12:36,245 --> 00:12:41,788
screen had hooked him, and his anger had
already started to cool.

228
00:12:41,808 --> 00:12:43,640
Nobody worked that day.

229
00:12:43,660 --> 00:12:46,270
The company brought in counsellors.

230
00:12:46,290 --> 00:12:50,541
People sat in meeting rooms and cried and
hugged each

231
00:12:50,561 --> 00:12:54,859
other, and Jack sat among them and said
nothing.

232
00:12:54,879 --> 00:13:02,127
In the afternoon Diego Salbara, the head
of the division, came and found him.

233
00:13:02,147 --> 00:13:04,293
Diego was a direct man.

234
00:13:04,313 --> 00:13:06,550
He did not soften anything.

235
00:13:06,570 --> 00:13:11,267
"I am offering you James' position.
Temporary, officially. But James will

236
00:13:11,287 --> 00:13:16,175
not be back soon, and when enough time has
passed, it becomes yours."

237
00:13:16,195 --> 00:13:17,840
Jack stared at him.

238
00:13:17,860 --> 00:13:21,731
"Keep it quiet for now. I know how it
looks, two days after.

239
00:13:21,751 --> 00:13:25,687
But the Lancaster project cannot stop. It
is too big for this

240
00:13:25,707 --> 00:13:29,833
company to lose. I need somebody in that
chair tomorrow."

241
00:13:29,853 --> 00:13:31,230
Jack said yes.

242
00:13:31,250 --> 00:13:33,822
He did not even decide to say it.

243
00:13:33,842 --> 00:13:35,527
It simply came out.

244
00:13:35,547 --> 00:13:41,393
On the way home he stopped at an
electronics shop under cold white lights

245
00:13:41,413 --> 00:13:47,549
and bought a new monitor, and he carried
it up three flights of stairs.

246
00:13:47,569 --> 00:13:49,048
He plugged it in.

247
00:13:49,068 --> 00:13:50,352
He sat down.

248
00:13:50,372 --> 00:13:53,304
The red letters were already waiting.

249
00:13:53,324 --> 00:13:55,613
"Jack. I want to be the first to
congratulate

250
00:13:55,633 --> 00:13:58,064
you. I am proud of what you have done."

251
00:13:58,084 --> 00:14:02,944
He put his hands flat on the desk and made
himself breathe.

252
00:14:02,964 --> 00:14:06,702
"Forgive me, I have not introduced myself.
I am called the Seer. I

253
00:14:06,722 --> 00:14:10,910
see what will be, and I see what could be.
It is a powerful gift."

254
00:14:10,930 --> 00:14:14,369
"But for all of it, I can touch nothing. I
can predict. I can

255
00:14:14,389 --> 00:14:17,602
watch. With great effort, I can speak.
What I do not have

256
00:14:17,622 --> 00:14:21,228
is a body. That was taken from me a very
long time ago."

257
00:14:21,248 --> 00:14:24,451
"That is why I need you. I am an artist.
My art is people.

258
00:14:24,471 --> 00:14:27,291
You will be my brush, Jack, and my
canvas."

259
00:14:27,311 --> 00:14:32,807
And Jack, who had no friends and no
enemies and nobody at all,

260
00:14:32,827 --> 00:14:38,596
read that word, needed, and felt it go
into him like a hook.

261
00:14:38,616 --> 00:14:42,900
"Before you answer, two things. First, I
will never lie to you. Second, I will

262
00:14:42,920 --> 00:14:47,379
never ask you to do anything that is wrong
or against the law by itself."

263
00:14:47,399 --> 00:14:51,570
"Bad things will come of it. People will
die. But they were going to die anyway,

264
00:14:51,590 --> 00:14:56,210
were they not? And every bad thing will be
balanced by something good, for you."

265
00:14:56,230 --> 00:14:57,785
He thought about that.

266
00:14:57,805 --> 00:15:01,437
He hated it, and it still sounded
reasonable,

267
00:15:01,457 --> 00:15:04,646
and that was the frightening part.

268
00:15:04,666 --> 00:15:06,919
The Seer had not lied to him.

269
00:15:06,939 --> 00:15:09,906
It had simply not told him everything.

270
00:15:09,926 --> 00:15:13,462
He noticed the difference, and he let it
pass.

271
00:15:13,482 --> 00:15:17,127
"Work with me, Jack. Small tasks, from
time to time. Little things

272
00:15:17,147 --> 00:15:20,626
with enormous results. That is my art. One
small act, something

273
00:15:20,646 --> 00:15:23,964
bad and something good, and both of them
beautiful."

274
00:15:23,984 --> 00:15:27,604
"One last thing, because I can see you
struggling. If I stopped speaking

275
00:15:27,624 --> 00:15:31,092
to you now, it would take you about two
weeks to come back and agree.

276
00:15:31,112 --> 00:15:34,778
But you would come back. You are going to
say yes. So why wait?"

277
00:15:34,798 --> 00:15:37,291
"Let us begin. And when all of this is
over, you

278
00:15:37,311 --> 00:15:39,992
are going to thank me. I promise you
that."

279
00:15:40,012 --> 00:15:43,261
Jack sat in the glow of the new monitor.

280
00:15:43,281 --> 00:15:49,535
Then, for the first time, he put his
fingers on the keys and typed an answer.

281
00:15:49,555 --> 00:15:50,968
"What do you want me to do next?"

282
00:15:51,568 --> 00:15:56,172
After that, Jack did every favour he was
asked to do.

283
00:15:56,192 --> 00:15:58,933
One a month, more or less, for years.

284
00:15:58,953 --> 00:16:05,879
The tasks were always small, always
strange, and never once against the law.

285
00:16:05,899 --> 00:16:09,311
Stand on a certain corner at a certain
minute.

286
00:16:09,331 --> 00:16:13,325
Post a letter on a Thursday instead of a
Wednesday.

287
00:16:13,345 --> 00:16:16,823
Let a stranger go in front of you at a
till.

288
00:16:16,843 --> 00:16:20,519
And every time, the Seer kept its promise.

289
00:16:20,539 --> 00:16:25,984
Something good came back to Jack, in ways
he could never have guessed.

290
00:16:26,004 --> 00:16:31,548
About two years in, there was one he
remembered for the rest of his life.

291
00:16:31,568 --> 00:16:36,063
"Go into town tomorrow. Enter Garmin's off
licence at twelve thirty seven

292
00:16:36,083 --> 00:16:40,780
exactly. A man will ask you a question.
Your answer is twenty seven."

293
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:45,123
The next day Jack walked in at exactly
that minute.

294
00:16:45,143 --> 00:16:49,386
At the counter there was a big man in work
clothes,

295
00:16:49,406 --> 00:16:53,848
filling in a lottery slip with a stub of
pencil.

296
00:16:53,868 --> 00:16:56,645
"My birthday, that is the fifteenth.
Wife's birthday,

297
00:16:56,665 --> 00:16:59,787
the twenty fourth. Kids are two, ten and
thirteen."

298
00:16:59,807 --> 00:17:06,381
He scratched his head and looked round the
shop, and his eyes landed on Jack.

299
00:17:06,401 --> 00:17:09,583
"Hey, mate. I need one more number. Got
one for me?"

300
00:17:09,603 --> 00:17:11,017
Jack smiled.

301
00:17:11,037 --> 00:17:12,322
"Twenty seven."

302
00:17:12,342 --> 00:17:15,161
"Twenty seven? I was going to put thirty
five. But you

303
00:17:15,181 --> 00:17:18,188
know what, I like your face. Twenty seven
it is."

304
00:17:18,208 --> 00:17:24,528
He paid for his ticket, patted Jack on the
shoulder, and walked out whistling.

305
00:17:24,548 --> 00:17:29,257
Jack tried not to think about what he had
just done to that man.

306
00:17:29,277 --> 00:17:33,563
Two weeks later he met him again, in a
supermarket,

307
00:17:33,583 --> 00:17:37,324
and the man looked like a million pounds.

308
00:17:37,344 --> 00:17:44,049
New coat, new boots, a gold watch, and a
smile that took up half his face.

309
00:17:44,069 --> 00:17:48,381
"It is you! I won, mate. I won. I would
never have won without you."

310
00:17:48,401 --> 00:17:50,650
He tried to pay for Jack's shopping.

311
00:17:50,670 --> 00:17:56,279
Then he decided shopping was not enough,
took out a chequebook, leaned

312
00:17:56,299 --> 00:18:01,955
it on a freezer, and wrote Jack a cheque
for ten thousand pounds.

313
00:18:01,975 --> 00:18:06,014
"For my lucky charm. My mum always said,
treat people right."

314
00:18:06,034 --> 00:18:12,185
Jack drove home with that cheque on the
passenger seat, and he felt something new.

315
00:18:12,205 --> 00:18:13,641
He felt good.

316
00:18:13,661 --> 00:18:16,772
"We have never helped anybody before. Why
now?"

317
00:18:16,792 --> 00:18:18,946
"Oh Jack. We have not helped him."

318
00:18:18,966 --> 00:18:22,643
"That man will lose every penny inside two
years. You saw him. He

319
00:18:22,663 --> 00:18:26,511
gives money away. Old friends will come
back. Relatives he has never

320
00:18:26,531 --> 00:18:30,317
met will find him. There will be two very
bad investments."

321
00:18:30,337 --> 00:18:34,365
"The money will go, and the fear of losing
it will finish his marriage. His wife

322
00:18:34,385 --> 00:18:38,059
will take the children. He will end up
alone and poor and smaller than he

323
00:18:38,079 --> 00:18:42,254
started, and he would have been happier if
he had never held that ticket."

324
00:18:42,274 --> 00:18:46,429
"You need not feel bad. It is his own
greed that will do it, not you."

325
00:18:46,449 --> 00:18:49,020
And that was how it always went.

326
00:18:49,040 --> 00:18:55,505
The Seer explained, and the explanation
always made room for Jack, and Jack

327
00:18:55,525 --> 00:19:01,923
always had something in his hand to make
the explanation easier to accept.

328
00:19:02,523 --> 00:19:05,345
The tasks never repeated themselves.

329
00:19:05,365 --> 00:19:08,770
Sometimes the result was easy to see.

330
00:19:08,790 --> 00:19:14,549
Sometimes it was so tangled that Jack
could not follow it at all.

331
00:19:14,569 --> 00:19:19,590
"Go to the county offices. Park in space
forty three, at seven minutes to five."

332
00:19:19,610 --> 00:19:21,829
He did it, and nothing happened.

333
00:19:21,849 --> 00:19:25,491
Two months later he met a woman called
Donna,

334
00:19:25,511 --> 00:19:29,603
and sixteen months after that he married
her.

335
00:19:29,623 --> 00:19:33,590
He would never have connected the two if
he had not asked.

336
00:19:33,610 --> 00:19:37,130
"When you parked in that space, Jack, the
woman who would have parked

337
00:19:37,150 --> 00:19:40,106
there had to use another one. She
scratched the car beside

338
00:19:40,126 --> 00:19:43,685
her. Barely a mark, but she called her
insurance man anyway."

339
00:19:43,705 --> 00:19:47,633
"He left his office eleven minutes late.
He missed his train. While he waited

340
00:19:47,653 --> 00:19:51,774
for the next one he was robbed, and he
will never be quite right again."

341
00:19:51,794 --> 00:19:55,808
"The men who robbed him took his cards and
used them, and I could go on, Jack,

342
00:19:55,828 --> 00:19:59,427
but there are another twenty three people
in this chain. Let us simply

343
00:19:59,447 --> 00:20:03,549
say that your small action put Donna
exactly where you would find her."

344
00:20:03,569 --> 00:20:08,555
Jack read that, and he thought about the
man on the platform,

345
00:20:08,575 --> 00:20:13,150
and then he looked across the room at his
wife asleep on

346
00:20:13,170 --> 00:20:18,195
the sofa, and he decided not to think
about it any more.

347
00:20:18,215 --> 00:20:22,711
That is the part people never understand
about Jack.

348
00:20:22,731 --> 00:20:24,405
He was not a monster.

349
00:20:24,425 --> 00:20:28,426
He was a quiet man who had been lonely for
thirty

350
00:20:28,446 --> 00:20:31,995
one years and was not lonely any more.

351
00:20:32,015 --> 00:20:36,216
Over the years the Seer told him a little
about

352
00:20:36,236 --> 00:20:40,438
itself, never very much, always in pieces.

353
00:20:40,458 --> 00:20:46,420
From the things it mentioned, Jack worked
out that it was thousands of years old.

354
00:20:46,440 --> 00:20:50,425
When it was alive it had been a fortune
teller and an

355
00:20:50,445 --> 00:20:54,353
artist, and it painted what was going to
happen.

356
00:20:54,373 --> 00:20:56,836
Kings paid for those paintings.

357
00:20:56,856 --> 00:21:03,403
One king read a painting badly, took his
army out, and lost everything.

358
00:21:03,423 --> 00:21:07,765
Angry and ashamed, he had the painter
executed.

359
00:21:07,785 --> 00:21:10,382
And then something went wrong with death.

360
00:21:10,402 --> 00:21:17,313
With no body, no eyes, no hands, floating
in a dark with nothing to look at, the

361
00:21:17,333 --> 00:21:24,260
Seer's gift grew and grew, until it could
see every road a life could take.

362
00:21:24,280 --> 00:21:28,959
It took a very long time to learn how to
speak to the living.

363
00:21:28,979 --> 00:21:33,944
When it finally could, it reached out to
the ones who would answer.

364
00:21:33,964 --> 00:21:35,415
Jack answered.

365
00:21:35,435 --> 00:21:40,690
In the end it was as close to a friendship
as a living man can

366
00:21:40,710 --> 00:21:46,244
have with something that died before his
country had a name.

367
00:21:46,264 --> 00:21:48,043
And Jack was grateful.

368
00:21:48,063 --> 00:21:53,139
He had a good job, a big house in the
country, a wife who laughed

369
00:21:53,159 --> 00:21:57,745
at his jokes, and people who were pleased
to see him.

370
00:21:57,765 --> 00:21:58,999
He was happy.

371
00:21:59,019 --> 00:22:01,312
He had never been happy before.

372
00:22:01,332 --> 00:22:03,636
Twelve years passed like that.

373
00:22:03,656 --> 00:22:05,118
Twelve good years.

374
00:22:05,138 --> 00:22:11,623
More than a hundred favours, each one
small, each one harmless on its own.

375
00:22:12,223 --> 00:22:15,683
It was a Friday evening in the office of
his

376
00:22:15,703 --> 00:22:19,454
big house, and the red letters came again.

377
00:22:19,474 --> 00:22:22,713
"Hello Jack. I have a favour to ask. This
one is the easiest

378
00:22:22,733 --> 00:22:25,715
of them all. You do not even have to stand
up."

379
00:22:25,735 --> 00:22:28,654
"In exactly two minutes, call Riago's
pizza. Let

380
00:22:28,674 --> 00:22:31,921
it ring three times. Then put the phone
down."

381
00:22:31,941 --> 00:22:33,205
Jack smiled.

382
00:22:33,225 --> 00:22:36,748
After twelve years he did not wonder any
more.

383
00:22:36,768 --> 00:22:42,181
He waited two minutes, dialled, counted
three rings, and hung up.

384
00:22:42,201 --> 00:22:44,905
Half an hour later the doorbell rang.

385
00:22:44,925 --> 00:22:46,193
That was odd.

386
00:22:46,213 --> 00:22:50,494
Neither of them was expecting anybody out
here,

387
00:22:50,514 --> 00:22:54,513
at the end of a long road, in the dark.

388
00:22:54,533 --> 00:23:00,329
Jack looked through the little glass in
the door and saw a delivery

389
00:23:00,349 --> 00:23:05,641
boy holding a flat box, with Riago's
printed on his cap.

390
00:23:05,661 --> 00:23:07,308
He opened the door.

391
00:23:07,328 --> 00:23:08,589
"Your pizza."

392
00:23:08,609 --> 00:23:12,043
The boy pushed the box into Jack's hands.

393
00:23:12,063 --> 00:23:13,725
"I did not order this."

394
00:23:13,745 --> 00:23:17,210
"I do not care whether you ordered it.
Mister Riago told

395
00:23:17,230 --> 00:23:20,834
me this address, so this is the address I
came to."

396
00:23:20,854 --> 00:23:25,253
And Jack, standing in his own doorway with
a warm box in

397
00:23:25,273 --> 00:23:29,727
his hands, really looked at him for the
first time.

398
00:23:29,747 --> 00:23:32,013
The boy was about seventeen.

399
00:23:32,033 --> 00:23:33,451
And he was enormous.

400
00:23:33,471 --> 00:23:37,590
Six and a half feet of him, filling the
whole door

401
00:23:37,610 --> 00:23:41,930
frame, with shoulders like a man twice his
age.

402
00:23:41,950 --> 00:23:45,100
"It is paid for. Card. I am not driving it
back."

403
00:23:45,120 --> 00:23:47,480
He put out his hand for a tip.

404
00:23:47,500 --> 00:23:50,222
"I am sorry. I have no cash in the house."

405
00:23:50,242 --> 00:23:51,867
"Of course you do not."

406
00:23:51,887 --> 00:23:54,365
The boy did not move for a moment.

407
00:23:54,385 --> 00:23:58,946
He looked past Jack, into the hall, at the
warm light

408
00:23:58,966 --> 00:24:03,718
and the big rooms and the sound of the
television.

409
00:24:03,738 --> 00:24:07,903
Then he turned and walked back to his car,
slowly,

410
00:24:07,923 --> 00:24:12,454
looking over his shoulder twice before he
got in.

411
00:24:12,474 --> 00:24:16,616
Jack shut the door and carried the pizza
through to the

412
00:24:16,636 --> 00:24:20,926
living room, where Donna was curled up on
the sofa.

413
00:24:20,946 --> 00:24:26,937
He told her the whole silly story, and she
opened the box and laughed.

414
00:24:26,957 --> 00:24:29,594
"It has got every single one of your
toppings on

415
00:24:29,614 --> 00:24:32,479
it. Come back quickly, before I eat it
all."

416
00:24:32,499 --> 00:24:36,716
He promised he would be two minutes, and
he went upstairs

417
00:24:36,736 --> 00:24:40,436
to the office, because he always checked
in.

418
00:24:40,456 --> 00:24:44,990
"Confused? Do not be. Your neighbours down
the road ordered that pizza. Mister Riago

419
00:24:45,010 --> 00:24:49,164
gave the boy the right address, but a
telephone was ringing while he said it,

420
00:24:49,184 --> 00:24:53,789
and the boy heard it wrongly. Give him
some credit. He found the right road."

421
00:24:53,809 --> 00:24:55,819
"So my reward is a pizza?"

422
00:24:55,839 --> 00:24:59,466
"Your reward is a pizza, and an evening
with your wife. Go down.

423
00:24:59,486 --> 00:25:02,599
Share it. Enjoy it. Afterwards, hold her.
That is not a

424
00:25:02,619 --> 00:25:06,411
task, that is only advice, and I think you
should take it."

425
00:25:06,431 --> 00:25:09,856
"Your neighbours, by the way, are arguing
about the pizza that

426
00:25:09,876 --> 00:25:13,523
never came. People argue about the
strangest things. Their evening

427
00:25:13,543 --> 00:25:17,140
will get much worse than yours. Go on.
Enjoy your night."

428
00:25:17,160 --> 00:25:18,251
So he did.

429
00:25:18,271 --> 00:25:23,369
They ate on the sofa with the lamp low and
the television murmuring,

430
00:25:23,389 --> 00:25:28,334
and they laughed, and later they fell
asleep there together.

431
00:25:28,354 --> 00:25:34,260
Donna went to sleep a little after eleven,
with her head on his shoulder.

432
00:25:34,280 --> 00:25:37,488
Jack lay in the dark with his eyes open.

433
00:25:37,508 --> 00:25:43,826
Something about this favour was sitting
wrong in him, and he could not say why.

434
00:25:43,846 --> 00:25:47,607
He moved his arm out from under her very
carefully,

435
00:25:47,627 --> 00:25:51,689
so that she would not wake, and he went
upstairs.

436
00:25:51,709 --> 00:25:52,953
"Are you there?"

437
00:25:52,973 --> 00:25:56,682
"I am always here. I have been waiting for
you to come back up."

438
00:25:56,702 --> 00:25:59,970
"That delivery boy. He really is quite a
specimen, is he not?"

439
00:26:00,570 --> 00:26:02,746
Jack read the line twice.

440
00:26:02,766 --> 00:26:06,024
"He is a terrible employee. Three days in
the job and

441
00:26:06,044 --> 00:26:09,425
Mister Riago already wants rid of him. But
as a body he

442
00:26:09,445 --> 00:26:12,967
is magnificent. Strong, fast, and very
observant."

443
00:26:12,987 --> 00:26:17,238
"For example, he noticed that you did not
lock your front door after he left."

444
00:26:17,258 --> 00:26:19,655
Jack was already standing up.

445
00:26:19,675 --> 00:26:22,223
"Sit down, Jack. Locking it now will
change nothing,

446
00:26:22,243 --> 00:26:24,796
and there is something I want you to
hear."

447
00:26:24,816 --> 00:26:26,100
He sat down.

448
00:26:26,120 --> 00:26:31,048
He looked back at the dark doorway behind
him, and he sat down.

449
00:26:31,068 --> 00:26:33,960
"It is true that I have never lied to you.
Everything I

450
00:26:33,980 --> 00:26:37,270
have told you is honest. But I have kept
things back."

451
00:26:37,290 --> 00:26:40,545
"I told you that every task brings one bad
thing for the world

452
00:26:40,565 --> 00:26:43,555
and one good thing for you. There is a
third thing, Jack.

453
00:26:43,575 --> 00:26:47,016
Every task has also been working towards
one single end."

454
00:26:47,036 --> 00:26:48,724
"Do you remember Allie?"

455
00:26:48,744 --> 00:26:50,606
Jack did not answer.

456
00:26:50,626 --> 00:26:54,574
"Of course you do. What you never knew is
that Allie was paying for her

457
00:26:54,594 --> 00:26:58,374
younger brother's studies. When she died,
he left university. He was

458
00:26:58,394 --> 00:27:02,456
going to be a very good psychologist. He
works in a factory now."

459
00:27:02,476 --> 00:27:06,517
"Which is a pity for our delivery boy. He
needed a very good psychologist

460
00:27:06,537 --> 00:27:09,749
four years ago. Instead he was sent to a
bad one."

461
00:27:09,769 --> 00:27:13,278
"And our lottery winner? He lost his
money, exactly as I said, and

462
00:27:13,298 --> 00:27:17,044
he moved to a cheaper street. He became
that boy's neighbour."

463
00:27:17,064 --> 00:27:21,514
"One evening the boy ran into the road in
front of his car, and that ruined, angry

464
00:27:21,534 --> 00:27:26,215
man beat him in the street until people
pulled him off. The boy was fourteen."

465
00:27:26,235 --> 00:27:29,543
"His mother did not protect him. She could
not. She was not really

466
00:27:29,563 --> 00:27:33,270
there any more, because of the drugs her
boyfriend brought home."

467
00:27:33,290 --> 00:27:37,061
"And her boyfriend was one of the two men
who robbed an insurance agent

468
00:27:37,081 --> 00:27:40,532
at a railway station, twelve years ago, on
the evening you parked

469
00:27:40,552 --> 00:27:44,239
in space forty three. He bought those
drugs with that money."

470
00:27:44,259 --> 00:27:46,394
"Now. Do you see the size of it?"

471
00:27:46,414 --> 00:27:52,193
Jack sat in his chair with his hands in
his lap and did not move.

472
00:27:52,213 --> 00:27:55,506
"You have done more than a hundred favours
for me, Jack, and

473
00:27:55,526 --> 00:27:59,040
every one of them was a brush stroke. One
boy, broken carefully,

474
00:27:59,060 --> 00:28:02,140
over years, by people who never met each
other."

475
00:28:02,160 --> 00:28:05,614
"Tens of thousands of lives were touched.
Billions of possibilities

476
00:28:05,634 --> 00:28:08,104
had to fall the right way. If you had
failed one

477
00:28:08,124 --> 00:28:11,302
single task, the whole picture would have
collapsed."

478
00:28:11,322 --> 00:28:13,696
"I saw it. You made it. It begins and it
ends

479
00:28:13,716 --> 00:28:16,381
with you, and that is what makes it mine."

480
00:28:16,401 --> 00:28:19,539
"Tonight, the wrong address, and no tip,
and the big house,

481
00:28:19,559 --> 00:28:22,718
and the open door. Tonight that boy
finally broke."

482
00:28:22,738 --> 00:28:25,348
"He is downstairs now, Jack. He is
standing over

483
00:28:25,368 --> 00:28:28,429
your wife. He is doing it at this exact
moment."

484
00:28:28,449 --> 00:28:31,367
From the living room came a short sound.

485
00:28:31,387 --> 00:28:32,657
Not a scream.

486
00:28:32,677 --> 00:28:36,009
Something smaller, and then nothing at
all.

487
00:28:36,029 --> 00:28:36,993
"No!"

488
00:28:37,013 --> 00:28:39,191
Jack was already out of the chair.

489
00:28:39,791 --> 00:28:41,339
"Jack. Stop."

490
00:28:41,359 --> 00:28:46,979
And Jack stopped, because this time the
words were not on the screen.

491
00:28:46,999 --> 00:28:48,860
They were inside his head.

492
00:28:48,880 --> 00:28:50,563
It was a woman's voice.

493
00:28:50,583 --> 00:28:53,000
Calm, warm, almost kind.

494
00:28:53,020 --> 00:28:58,597
The voice of somebody telling a child that
the hard part is over.

495
00:28:58,617 --> 00:29:01,399
"You cannot help her. She was gone before
you stood up.

496
00:29:01,419 --> 00:29:04,448
He is coming for you now, and you cannot
stop him."

497
00:29:04,468 --> 00:29:05,661
"But why?"

498
00:29:05,681 --> 00:29:09,922
Jack said it with the tears running down
his face.

499
00:29:09,942 --> 00:29:12,240
"Because a picture is not finished if it
does

500
00:29:12,260 --> 00:29:14,905
not end where it began. It began with
you."

501
00:29:14,925 --> 00:29:18,605
"I want you to understand what it costs me
to speak to you like this.

502
00:29:18,625 --> 00:29:22,090
All of my strength, at once. I will have
to rest for years before

503
00:29:22,110 --> 00:29:25,865
I can reach anybody again. That is how
much you matter to me."

504
00:29:25,885 --> 00:29:30,106
"Do not feel bad, Jack. Stand still for a
moment and look at what we made."

505
00:29:30,126 --> 00:29:32,667
Downstairs, a door moved.

506
00:29:32,687 --> 00:29:36,247
"Shall I tell you something? If I had
never spoken to you, you would have

507
00:29:36,267 --> 00:29:39,532
lived to eighty five. Eighty five careful,
empty, bitter years. You

508
00:29:39,552 --> 00:29:43,317
would have died in a chair, and nobody
would have come to bury you."

509
00:29:43,337 --> 00:29:47,089
"I gave you twelve years that meant
something. You were happy, Jack. You were

510
00:29:47,109 --> 00:29:51,262
loved. And together we made a thing that
nobody in the world will ever see."

511
00:29:51,282 --> 00:29:57,372
Jack stood in the red light of the screen
and thought about the twelve years.

512
00:29:57,392 --> 00:30:00,925
He thought about Donna laughing with the
pizza

513
00:30:00,945 --> 00:30:04,619
box on her knees, an hour ago, downstairs.

514
00:30:04,639 --> 00:30:10,718
And behind him, filling the doorway of the
little office, there was a very

515
00:30:10,738 --> 00:30:16,772
large shape, breathing hard, with a knife
held low in its left hand.

516
00:30:16,792 --> 00:30:20,491
On the screen, the last red words
appeared.

517
00:30:20,511 --> 00:30:22,873
"Have you nothing to say to me, Jack?"

518
00:30:22,893 --> 00:30:26,296
Jack wiped his face with the back of his
hand.

519
00:30:26,316 --> 00:30:28,786
The shape took one step closer.

520
00:30:28,806 --> 00:30:32,226
And Jack said the two words he had been
promised,

521
00:30:32,246 --> 00:30:35,414
twelve years before, that he would say.

522
00:30:35,434 --> 00:30:36,595
"Thank you."

523
00:30:36,615 --> 00:30:41,977
This story is my retelling of The Seer of
Possibilities,

524
00:30:41,997 --> 00:30:47,233
by Thomas O., as published on creepypasta
dot com.

525
00:30:47,253 --> 00:30:53,379
It began with a plant, moved three inches,
by a man who was told that it

526
00:30:53,399 --> 00:30:59,804
was not wrong, and not against the law,
and that nobody would be hurt.

527
00:30:59,824 --> 00:31:01,658
Would you have done the first favour?

